Day 53 - Linked List Cycle

Problem Statement:
Given head, the head of a linked list, determine if the linked list has a cycle in it.

There is a cycle if some node can be reached again by continuously following the next pointer.

Return true if the linked list contains a cycle. Otherwise, return false.

Code

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(NULL) {}
 * };
 */


class Solution {
public:
    bool hasCycle(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* fast = head;
        ListNode* slow = head;


        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;


            if (slow == fast) {
                return true;
            }
        }


        return false;
    }
};



Time Complexity:
O(n)

Space Complexity:
O(1)

Approach:
This solution uses Floyd's Cycle Detection Algorithm, also known as the Tortoise and Hare algorithm. Two pointers are used: slow moves one node at a time, while fast moves two nodes at a time. If there is no cycle, fast will eventually reach NULL. If a cycle exists, fast will eventually meet slow inside the cycle. Therefore, if slow == fast, we return true; otherwise, after reaching the end of the list, we return false.

